1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,852
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:08,280
This is chapter thirty-five: memory footprint and object layout.

3
00:00:08,730 --> 00:00:12,490
Java hides memory layout, which is usually a feature.

4
00:00:12,490 --> 00:00:18,803
It stops being one when a million small objects take four times the memory you estimated.

5
00:00:18,803 --> 00:00:20,506
Let's see where it goes.

6
00:00:21,406 --> 00:00:24,671
The SKU catalogue holds a million entries.

7
00:00:24,671 --> 00:00:30,113
The estimate: four plus four plus eight bytes of fields, plus the SKU.

8
00:00:30,113 --> 00:00:31,667
Say fifty megabytes.

9
00:00:32,117 --> 00:00:36,284
The class histogram says two hundred and sixteen megabytes.

10
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:37,484
Nothing is wrong.

11
00:00:37,484 --> 00:00:47,371
The estimate ignored object headers, references, the map's own nodes, and the fact that a SKU wrapping a String is several objects, not one.

12
00:00:48,271 --> 00:00:50,725
Every object starts with a header.

13
00:00:50,725 --> 00:00:55,417
An eight-byte mark word, holding the hash, lock state and GC age.

14
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:58,875
Then a class pointer, four bytes when compressed.

15
00:00:59,325 --> 00:01:01,346
That's twelve bytes of header.

16
00:01:01,346 --> 00:01:04,040
Java 25's compact headers make it eight.

17
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:07,519
Each reference field is four bytes, compressed.

18
00:01:07,969 --> 00:01:11,102
And everything rounds up to a multiple of eight.

19
00:01:11,102 --> 00:01:14,561
So even an object with no fields takes sixteen bytes.

20
00:01:15,011 --> 00:01:21,482
Here's the idea that drives every optimisation: object count matters more than field count.

21
00:01:21,482 --> 00:01:26,531
Each reference hop adds another header, and each map entry adds a node.

22
00:01:27,431 --> 00:01:29,243
Some sizes worth knowing.

23
00:01:29,243 --> 00:01:32,214
An Object or an Integer is sixteen bytes.

24
00:01:32,214 --> 00:01:34,387
A Long or Double, twenty-four.

25
00:01:34,387 --> 00:01:38,010
An empty String is forty, counting its byte array.

26
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:40,981
Arrays are sixteen plus four per element.

27
00:01:40,981 --> 00:01:48,082
A HashMap node is thirty-two, and a LinkedList node is twenty-four, plus the element it points to.

28
00:01:48,532 --> 00:01:53,932
And to measure one object exactly, use JOL, the Java Object Layout library.

29
00:01:53,932 --> 00:01:57,244
It shows each field's offset, and the padding.

30
00:01:57,244 --> 00:02:00,052
Here, eight of forty bytes are padding.

31
00:02:00,952 --> 00:02:03,726
Let's account for one catalogue entry.

32
00:02:03,726 --> 00:02:07,011
The InventoryItem record is thirty-two bytes.

33
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:09,743
The Sku wrapping the code is sixteen.

34
00:02:10,193 --> 00:02:13,884
The String is twenty-four, plus thirty-two for its byte array.

35
00:02:14,334 --> 00:02:19,241
Then the HashMap node, thirty-two, plus about five bytes of table slot.

36
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:26,528
That's around a hundred and forty-one bytes per entry, against sixteen bytes of actual fields.

37
00:02:26,528 --> 00:02:32,128
A nine-times multiplier, entirely headers, references and container overhead.

38
00:02:33,028 --> 00:02:35,477
Boxing is the most dramatic case.

39
00:02:35,477 --> 00:02:41,190
A HashMap of a million Integer keys and values is about sixty-nine megabytes.

40
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:44,900
A plain int array holding the same counts is four.

41
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:46,681
Seventeen times smaller.

42
00:02:47,131 --> 00:02:51,095
The general technique is removing objects, not fields.

43
00:02:51,095 --> 00:02:54,766
A record graph in a map is five objects per entry.

44
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:58,510
Parallel arrays with an index get that down to two.

45
00:02:58,510 --> 00:03:01,446
Arrays with encoded keys, close to zero.

46
00:03:01,896 --> 00:03:05,910
But flattened data is harder to read and easy to get wrong.

47
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:14,346
Only do it when a measurement says footprint is the constraint, and hide it behind an API that still speaks in domain terms.

48
00:03:14,346 --> 00:03:21,693
For big numeric maps, libraries like fastutil or Eclipse Collections give you primitive maps with no boxing.

49
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:23,151
Strings.

50
00:03:23,151 --> 00:03:33,051
Since Java 9, compact strings store ASCII text in one byte per character instead of two, roughly halving string memory, with no configuration.

51
00:03:33,051 --> 00:03:38,907
What's left is duplication: a million String objects with far fewer distinct values.

52
00:03:38,907 --> 00:03:46,785
Enabling G1 string deduplication costs nothing to try, and the measurement shows straight away whether it helped.

53
00:03:47,235 --> 00:03:52,608
And Java 25's compact object headers cut the header from twelve bytes to eight.

54
00:03:52,608 --> 00:04:03,491
A HashMap node drops from thirty-two to twenty-four, and our catalogue entry from about a hundred and forty-one to a hundred and seventeen, with no code change.

55
00:04:03,491 --> 00:04:06,755
So re-baseline your heap sizing after upgrading.

56
00:04:07,655 --> 00:04:16,253
Compressed references store a thirty-two-bit value scaled by the eight-byte alignment, which addresses thirty-two gigabytes of heap.

57
00:04:16,703 --> 00:04:20,756
Go past that, and every reference doubles to sixty-four bits.

58
00:04:21,206 --> 00:04:27,488
So a thirty-three gigabyte heap can hold fewer objects than a thirty-one gigabyte heap.

59
00:04:27,488 --> 00:04:29,294
Check the flag with jcmd.

60
00:04:29,294 --> 00:04:34,854
Past the threshold, the answer is usually more JVMs rather than a bigger one.

61
00:04:34,854 --> 00:04:43,158
And note that the JVM reorders fields to minimise padding, so declaration order in your source makes no difference.

62
00:04:44,058 --> 00:04:44,711
The tools.

63
00:04:44,711 --> 00:04:48,303
The class histogram shows what's on the heap, by class.

64
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:52,124
A heap dump captures everything for offline analysis.

65
00:04:52,574 --> 00:04:56,158
JOL shows the exact layout of a single object.

66
00:04:56,608 --> 00:05:05,184
Eclipse Memory Analyzer shows retained sizes, meaning what would be freed if an object went away, and points at likely leaks.

67
00:05:05,634 --> 00:05:12,546
And native memory tracking shows the memory outside the heap, like metaspace, thread stacks and code cache.

68
00:05:13,446 --> 00:05:14,769
Production rules.

69
00:05:14,769 --> 00:05:17,958
Measure with the class histogram and JOL.

70
00:05:17,958 --> 00:05:20,759
Count objects per entry, not fields.

71
00:05:20,759 --> 00:05:24,571
Use primitive collections for large numeric maps.

72
00:05:24,571 --> 00:05:26,515
Try string deduplication.

73
00:05:26,515 --> 00:05:30,094
Stay under thirty-two gigabytes, or scale out.

74
00:05:30,094 --> 00:05:34,139
And re-baseline heap sizing after moving to Java 25.

75
00:05:34,589 --> 00:05:37,777
Never estimate memory from field sizes alone.

76
00:05:37,777 --> 00:05:40,681
Never flatten data without a measurement.

77
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:47,268
Don't reach for String dot intern casually, since interned strings are effectively permanent.

78
00:05:47,268 --> 00:05:51,022
Never pick a thirty-three gigabyte heap for headroom.

79
00:05:51,022 --> 00:05:56,051
And don't reorder fields to save padding, because the JVM already does.

80
00:05:56,951 --> 00:05:57,623
To recap.

81
00:05:57,623 --> 00:06:06,139
An object header is twelve bytes, eight on Java 25, everything aligns to eight, and the minimum object is sixteen.

82
00:06:06,139 --> 00:06:11,219
Object count drives footprint, often nine times the raw field bytes.

83
00:06:11,219 --> 00:06:14,506
Boxing and map nodes are the usual culprits.

84
00:06:14,506 --> 00:06:18,689
And compressed references end near thirty-two gigabytes.

85
00:06:19,139 --> 00:06:22,972
Next is chapter thirty-six: startup and Project Leyden.

86
00:06:22,972 --> 00:06:24,366
Thanks for watching.
